+## Spans: internal flow from another language (4.4.0)
+
+An in-process .NET run shows what happened *inside* each call it drew: the popup on an arrow holds the spans the
+service recorded while handling it, as an activity diagram and a flame chart, and each scenario's whole flow is a tab
+beside its sequence diagram ([[Internal Flow Tracking]]). From 4.4.0 an ingest draws the same from the spans the
+services under test exported, whatever language they are in. [[Ingesting from Jest|Ingesting-From-Jest]] is a worked
+harness for a Node service.
+
+```bash
+kronikol ingest ./captures --tests ./captures/tests.ndjson --spans ./captures/spans.jsonl -o ./Reports
+```
+
+**The file.** OTLP/JSON lines: one `TracesData` object (`{"resourceSpans":[…]}`) per line, as OpenTelemetry JS's
+`JsonTraceSerializer` (what its OTLP/HTTP JSON exporter sends) and the Collector's file exporter write them; a file
+holding one such document over several lines is read too. Ids in hex or base64, times in nanoseconds as strings or
+numbers, kinds as numbers or names: what [[Integration Otlp Extension]]'s reader accepts. Each span becomes the seven
+things internal flow draws: its trace id, span id and parent, its name, its instrumentation scope (the diagram's
+swimlane, else its service), its start and its duration. A line that is not a `TracesData` object, or holds a span
+with no trace id or span id, is skipped and counted as a [torn line](#torn-lines-a-killed-capturer-must-not-cost-you-the-report)
+is, and `--strict` fails on it.
+
+**The join.** A call takes the spans of the trace its `activityTraceId` names that started from 50 ms before its
+request to its response, as an in-process call does (`plans/SPAN_ATTRIBUTION_PLAN.md`): a capturer stamps each call
+with the trace and span it was made under (in OpenTelemetry JS, `trace.getActiveSpan().spanContext()` where the call
+is seen). When calls of two tests share a trace, each takes only the spans under its own `activitySpanId`, and a span
+both would take goes to neither, its popup saying so. A test's whole flow is every span of the traces only its calls
+carry.
+
+**The rules.**
+
+- Spans given, internal flow is on for the run, whatever `InternalFlowTracking` says: an ingest turns it off only
+  because a capture holds no spans.
+- They are drawn instead of this process's span store, which is neither read nor written.
+- All of them are drawn. The default granularity's list of instrumentation is .NET's, and would drop every span of
+  another language; `InternalFlowSpanGranularity.Manual` with `InternalFlowActivitySources` naming scopes narrows them
+  to those.
+- Ids are lowercased, and a span given twice (the same trace id and span id) is drawn once.
+- A span naming itself as its parent is drawn as a root.
+
+**The diagnostics** ([Diagnostics](#diagnostics)), each a line of the command's output and an entry of the report's:
+
+- `Spans supplied to the ingest: N span(s).`, where an in-process run counts its span store.
+- `N call(s) carry an activityTraceId no supplied span has`: the likely harness mistake, a call stamped with a trace
+  the span files do not hold.
+- `N supplied span(s) are in no call's internal flow`, in two parts with the first names: spans of a trace no call
+  carries (a service's start-up, a left-out retry's calls), and spans of a carried trace that started outside the
+  time of every call carrying it (clocks apart, or work after the response) or that calls of two tests would both
+  take.
+- Spans supplied more than once, and spans with no ids.
+
+**The inputs.** A span file inside an input directory is not read as interactions; an OTLP file among the inputs
+that `--spans` did not name is skipped with one line saying to pass it with `--spans`, instead of a malformed line
+per span.
+
+**Programmatically**, `IngestRequest.Spans` takes the spans as `FlowSpan`s; [[Integration Otlp Extension]] reads
+the file:
+
+```csharp
+using Kronikol.Extensions.Otlp;
+
+var malformed = new List<MalformedLine>();
+var result = IngestPipeline.Run(new IngestRequest
+{
+    InteractionFiles = Directory.GetFiles("captures", "*.ndjson"),
+    TestsFile = "captures/tests.ndjson",
+    Options = options,
+    Spans = OtlpTraceReader.ReadJsonLines("captures/spans.jsonl", malformed).Select(s => s.ToFlowSpan()).ToList(),
+});
+```
+
+Null `Spans`, the default, ingests as before 4.4.0: no internal flow unless the options turn it on, and the report
+4.3.0 wrote.
+
 ## Merging the wire and the span views
 
 When a stack is captured from both sides — a [[proxy or TCP tap|Integration-ProxyTap-Extension]] that decodes the wire, and an [[OTLP tap|Integration-Otlp-Extension]] that reads the spans the services already export — the same call arrives twice: the wire record has the payload, the status and the hit/miss label but had to *guess* which test it belongs to; the span record carries the exact trace id but usually no payload. `kronikol ingest --merge-duplicates` (`IngestRequest.MergeDuplicateInteractions`, with `MergeOverlapThreshold`, default `0.8`) folds them into one arrow: the span's `testId`/`traceId`/`activityTraceId`/`activitySpanId` win, the wire's `content`/`statusCode`/label win, and the merged request carries an `x-kronikol-captured-by: wire + span` pseudo-header so the note says where the arrow came from.
