+## Ingesting from Jest
+
+A Node service tested in-process under Jest (the service built in the test, driven with `fastify.inject`, its
+dependencies faked with MSW) can have the report an in-process .NET run has, with no change to the service and none
+to the tests: sequence diagrams of every call the test made and every call the service made, step bars with the
+setup calls in a Setup partition, a note per `expect`, a retried test shown as its last attempt, and each call's
+**internal flow** from the service's OpenTelemetry spans (4.4.0). A harness of five files writes three kinds of
+file, and `kronikol ingest` makes the report from them.
+
+The worked example is a Fastify 5 and Mercurius 16 GraphQL service whose resolver calls a payment provider (faked by
+MSW) and a risk service (a local fake MSW passes through). Every file below ran as shown, and the report was checked,
+with Node 22.22, Jest 30.5, Fastify 5.12, Mercurius 16.10, MSW 2.11, the OpenTelemetry JS SDK 2.11,
+`@opentelemetry/instrumentation-graphql` 0.70, `@opentelemetry/instrumentation-undici` 0.32 and `@fastify/otel` 0.21
+(`plans/INGEST_FIDELITY_PLAN.harness/otel-jest/recipe` in the repository, run with Kronikol 4.4.0).
+
+> The pieces are the owner's to change: another web framework needs its own inbound capture point, another fake its
+> own outbound one. What has to hold is the contract each file writes, which [[Ingesting External Captures]]
+> documents.
+
+---
+
+## What the harness writes
+
+| File | Written by | What it holds |
+|---|---|---|
+| `kronikol-capture/tests.ndjson` | the Jest environment, and the `expect` wrapper | Each test's `start`, its steps, its assertions and its `end`, with `attempt` on each, from every worker ([the tests format](Ingesting-External-Captures#the-tests-format-optional)) |
+| `kronikol-capture/interactions-<worker>.ndjson` | the capture points | A request line and a response line per call, each stamped with the running test's id and with the trace and the span the call was made under ([the interaction format](Ingesting-External-Captures#the-interaction-format)) |
+| `kronikol-capture/spans-<worker>.jsonl` | the tracing setup, after each test file | The file's spans, one OTLP/JSON `TracesData` object per line ([Spans](Ingesting-External-Captures#spans-internal-flow-from-another-language-440)) |
+
+Not `.kronikol/`: that folder above the report holds Kronikol's [[history ledger|Cross-Run-History]], whose
+`history.jsonl` an input directory would take for a capture.
+
+## `jest.config.js`
+
+```js
+module.exports = {
+  testEnvironment: '<rootDir>/kronikol/environment.js',
+  setupFiles: ['<rootDir>/kronikol/setup.js'],
+  setupFilesAfterEnv: ['<rootDir>/kronikol/after-env.js'],
+  testMatch: ['<rootDir>/tests/*.test.js']
+}
+```
+
+## `kronikol/environment.js`: tests, steps and outcomes
+
+A custom environment sees every test event. Each test gets an id stable across runs (its file and full name), set
+on the test's global for the capture points; its `beforeEach` hooks are its `Given` step and its body its `When`
+step, written when the test ends so each carries its status; a retry (`jest.retryTimes`) is the next `attempt` of
+the same id, which the report shows as its last attempt with a `retry 1` label.
+
+```js
+'use strict'
+// Jest environment: one tests NDJSON line per test start, its steps and its end. Steps are kept until the
+// test ends so each carries its status: beforeEach is the Given step (setup), the test body the When step.
+const { TestEnvironment } = require('jest-environment-node')
+const crypto = require('node:crypto')
+const fs = require('node:fs')
+const path = require('node:path')
+
+const dir = process.env.KRONIKOL_DIR ?? 'kronikol-capture'
+
+function fullName (test) {
+  const names = [test.name]
+  for (let block = test.parent; block && block.name !== 'ROOT_DESCRIBE_BLOCK'; block = block.parent) names.unshift(block.name)
+  return names.join(' › ')
+}
+
+function message (error) {
+  const e = Array.isArray(error) ? error[0] : error
+  return e?.stack ?? e?.message ?? String(e)
+}
+
+class KronikolEnvironment extends TestEnvironment {
+  constructor (config, context) {
+    super(config, context)
+    this.file = path.relative(process.cwd(), context.testPath).split(path.sep).join('/')
+    this.steps = []
+    fs.mkdirSync(dir, { recursive: true })
+  }
+
+  write (record) {
+    // One line per append: lines this short are written whole even with every worker appending to the file.
+    fs.appendFileSync(path.join(dir, 'tests.ndjson'), JSON.stringify(record) + '\n')
+  }
+
+  step (keyword, text) {
+    this.steps.push({ keyword, text, started: Date.now(), timestamp: new Date().toISOString() })
+  }
+
+  async handleTestEvent (event, state) {
+    const test = event.test ?? state.currentlyRunningTest
+    if (!test) return
+    const name = fullName(test)
+    const testId = crypto.createHash('sha256').update(`${this.file}|${name}`).digest('hex').slice(0, 32)
+    const attempt = test.invocations
+    switch (event.name) {
+      case 'test_start':
+        this.steps = []
+        // The capture points stamp every record with it; the Fastify hook binds it to each request.
+        this.global.__kronikolTestId = testId
+        this.global.__kronikolAttempt = attempt
+        this.write({ event: 'start', testId, testName: name, feature: this.file, sourceFile: this.file, attempt, timestamp: new Date().toISOString() })
+        break
+      case 'hook_start':
+        // Every beforeEach hook of the test is the one Given step, timed from the first.
+        if (event.hook.type === 'beforeEach' && this.steps.length === 0) this.step('Given', 'the test is set up')
+        break
+      case 'test_fn_start':
+        this.step('When', 'the test runs')
+        break
+      case 'test_done': {
+        const failed = test.errors.length > 0
+        this.steps.forEach((step, i) => {
+          const last = i === this.steps.length - 1
+          const next = this.steps[i + 1]
+          this.write({
+            event: 'step', testId, keyword: step.keyword, text: step.text, attempt, timestamp: step.timestamp,
+            status: failed && last ? 'failed' : 'passed',
+            durationMs: (next?.started ?? Date.now()) - step.started,
+            error: failed && last ? message(test.errors[0]) : undefined
+          })
+        })
+        this.write({
+          event: 'end', testId, attempt, timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
+          status: failed ? 'failed' : 'passed', durationMs: test.duration,
+          error: failed ? message(test.errors[0]) : undefined
+        })
+        this.global.__kronikolTestId = undefined
+        this.global.__kronikolAttempt = undefined
+        break
+      }
+    }
+  }
+}
+
+module.exports = KronikolEnvironment
+```
+
+Jest reports a test's `beforeEach` hooks one `hook_start` each (a test with one `beforeEach` of its own saw two), so
+the first opens the step.
+
+## `kronikol/capture.js`: the calls
+
+Inbound, Fastify announces every instance it creates on the `fastify.initialization` diagnostics channel, so the
+harness adds its hooks there and no test registers anything. The `onRequest` hook runs the rest of the request
+inside an `AsyncLocalStorage` scope holding the test the request arrived under, so a call the service makes after the
+test moved on keeps its test. Outbound, MSW's lifecycle events see every call the service makes, mocked or passed
+through.
+
+Each call is stamped with `trace.getActiveSpan()`'s trace id and span id: the join to the spans. Under MSW the
+listener runs inside the span the service made the call in (the resolver's), and in the Fastify hook inside the
+request's.
+
+```js
+'use strict'
+// Capture points: every inbound request Fastify serves and every outbound call MSW sees becomes a
+// request line and a response line of interactions-<worker>.ndjson, stamped with the running test's id and
+// with the trace and span the call was made under, which joins it to the spans kronikol ingest --spans reads.
+const { AsyncLocalStorage } = require('node:async_hooks')
+const crypto = require('node:crypto')
+const dc = require('node:diagnostics_channel')
+const fs = require('node:fs')
+const path = require('node:path')
+const { trace } = require('@opentelemetry/api')
+
+const dir = process.env.KRONIKOL_DIR ?? 'kronikol-capture'
+const file = path.join(dir, `interactions-${process.env.JEST_WORKER_ID ?? '1'}.ndjson`)
+const SERVICE = process.env.KRONIKOL_SERVICE ?? 'Service'
+const requestScope = new AsyncLocalStorage()
+
+/** The test the request being served arrived under, else the test running now. */
+const testId = () => requestScope.getStore()?.testId ?? globalThis.__kronikolTestId ?? 'outside-any-test'
+
+function write (record) {
+  fs.mkdirSync(dir, { recursive: true })
+  fs.appendFileSync(file, JSON.stringify(record) + '\n')
+}
+
+/** The ids of the call: its own, its test's, and the span it is made under. */
+function begin () {
+  const span = trace.getActiveSpan()?.spanContext()
+  return { requestResponseId: crypto.randomUUID(), testId: testId(), activityTraceId: span?.traceId, activitySpanId: span?.spanId }
+}
+
+// Inbound: Fastify announces every instance it creates on this channel, so no test registers anything.
+function onFastify ({ fastify }) {
+  const uri = request => `http://${request.hostname}${request.url}`
+  const writeRequest = request => {
+    if (request.kronikol.written) return
+    request.kronikol.written = true
+    const { written, at, ...call } = request.kronikol
+    write({ ...call, type: 'Request', method: request.method, uri: uri(request), serviceName: SERVICE, callerName: 'Test',
+      content: request.body === undefined ? undefined : JSON.stringify(request.body), timestamp: at })
+  }
+  fastify.addHook('onRequest', (request, reply, done) => {
+    // Everything the service does for this request keeps the test it arrived under, even past the test's end.
+    requestScope.run({ testId: testId() }, () => {
+      request.kronikol = { ...begin(), at: new Date().toISOString() }
+      done()
+    })
+  })
+  // Written once the body is parsed, at the time the request arrived; onResponse writes it for one that never got here.
+  fastify.addHook('preHandler', (request, reply, done) => { writeRequest(request); done() })
+  fastify.addHook('onSend', (request, reply, payload, done) => {
+    request.kronikolBody = typeof payload === 'string' ? payload : undefined
+    done()
+  })
+  fastify.addHook('onResponse', (request, reply, done) => {
+    writeRequest(request)
+    const { written, at, ...call } = request.kronikol
+    // Timed once the service's own onResponse hooks have run, so the spans of its response work are the call's.
+    setImmediate(() => write({ ...call, type: 'Response', method: request.method, uri: uri(request), serviceName: SERVICE, callerName: 'Test',
+      statusCode: String(reply.statusCode), content: request.kronikolBody, timestamp: new Date().toISOString() }))
+    done()
+  })
+}
+dc.subscribe('fastify.initialization', onFastify)
+
+// Outbound: what MSW sees, mocked or passed through. name maps a host to the service it stands for.
+function captureMsw (server, name = host => host) {
+  const calls = new Map()
+  server.events.on('request:start', async ({ request, requestId }) => {
+    const at = new Date().toISOString()
+    const call = { ...begin(), method: request.method, uri: request.url, serviceName: name(new URL(request.url).host), callerName: SERVICE }
+    calls.set(requestId, call)
+    const content = await request.clone().text()
+    write({ ...call, type: 'Request', content: content || undefined, timestamp: at })
+  })
+  const respond = async ({ response, requestId }) => {
+    const call = calls.get(requestId)
+    if (!call) return
+    calls.delete(requestId)
+    const at = new Date().toISOString()
+    const content = await response.clone().text()
+    write({ ...call, type: 'Response', statusCode: String(response.status), content: content || undefined, timestamp: at })
+  }
+  server.events.on('response:mocked', respond)
+  server.events.on('response:bypass', respond)
+}
+
+function stopCapture () {
+  dc.unsubscribe('fastify.initialization', onFastify)
+}
+
+module.exports = { captureMsw, stopCapture }
+```
+
+The response line is timed in `setImmediate`, once the service's own `onResponse` hooks have started: written in
+the hook itself, Mercurius's `onSend` and `onResponse` hook spans started after the call's end, and the ingest said
+so ("5 of a trace a call carries, which started outside the time of every call carrying it").
+
+A failed outbound call is a response line with the error's type behind a bang as its `statusCode`
+(`"!TypeError"`) and its message in `error` ([a failed call](Ingesting-External-Captures#the-interaction-format)).
+This recipe's fakes never failed; the shape is the in-process one.
+
+## `kronikol/tracing.js`: the service's spans under Jest
+
+OpenTelemetry's documented setup gives one span under Jest, the passed-through call's: its instrumentations hook
+Node's `require`, and Jest loads modules through its own registry. What works (`plans/INGEST_FIDELITY_PLAN.md` S0):
+
+- **Fastify**: `@fastify/otel` with `registerOnInitialization: true`, which adds its plugin to each instance Fastify
+  announces on the diagnostics channel. The channel is process-wide, so the subscription is undone after each test
+  file (`after-env.js`), or the next file in the worker fails with `FastifyError: The decorator 'opentelemetry' has
+  already been added!`.
+- **GraphQL**: the instrumentation's patches applied by hand, through `getModuleDefinitions()`, to the modules Jest's
+  own `require` returns, before the service loads: Mercurius takes `graphql`'s functions when it is required.
+- **fetch**: undici's instrumentation, which listens on diagnostics channels, as is.
+
+```js
+'use strict'
+// Spans: an in-memory tracer provider and the instrumentations, applied the way Jest needs. Loaded from a
+// setupFiles entry, so it runs in each test file's module registry before the file and the service load.
+const { trace, context } = require('@opentelemetry/api')
+const { AsyncLocalStorageContextManager } = require('@opentelemetry/context-async-hooks')
+const { BasicTracerProvider, InMemorySpanExporter, SimpleSpanProcessor } = require('@opentelemetry/sdk-trace-base')
+const { registerInstrumentations } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation')
+const { UndiciInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-undici')
+const { GraphQLInstrumentation } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation-graphql')
+const { FastifyOtelInstrumentation } = require('@fastify/otel')
+
+// Spans need no test id: a call carries the trace it was made in, and that joins them.
+const exporter = new InMemorySpanExporter()
+const provider = new BasicTracerProvider({ spanProcessors: [new SimpleSpanProcessor(exporter)] })
+const contextManager = new AsyncLocalStorageContextManager()
+contextManager.enable()
+context.setGlobalContextManager(contextManager)
+trace.setGlobalTracerProvider(provider)
+
+// Fastify announces each new instance on a diagnostics channel; registerOnInitialization adds the plugin to it.
+// The channel is process-wide, so the subscription is undone after each test file (after-env.js).
+const fastifyOtel = new FastifyOtelInstrumentation({ registerOnInitialization: true })
+// undici (global fetch) reports on diagnostics channels too, which Jest's module registry does not hide.
+registerInstrumentations({ tracerProvider: provider, instrumentations: [new UndiciInstrumentation(), fastifyOtel] })
+
+// The GraphQL instrumentation hooks Node's require, which Jest bypasses: apply its patches to the modules
+// Jest's own require returns, before the service is loaded (Mercurius takes graphql's functions when required).
+const graphql = new GraphQLInstrumentation({ mergeItems: true })
+graphql.setTracerProvider(provider)
+for (const definition of graphql.getModuleDefinitions()) {
+  const version = require(`${definition.name}/package.json`).version
+  if (definition.patch) definition.patch(require(definition.name), version)
+  for (const file of definition.files ?? []) file.patch(require(file.name), version)
+}
+
+module.exports = { exporter, fastifyOtel }
+```
+
+## `kronikol/setup.js` and `kronikol/after-env.js`
+
+`setupFiles` runs in each test file's module registry before the file. `setupFilesAfterEnv` has `afterAll` and
+`expect`: after each test file it writes the file's spans as one line (`JsonTraceSerializer` is what OpenTelemetry's
+OTLP/HTTP JSON exporter sends) and undoes the process-wide subscriptions, and it wraps `expect` so each matcher call
+is an `assertion` record whose text is the test's own line.
+
+```js
+'use strict'
+// Jest setupFiles: the spans first, so their instrumentation is in place before anything loads the service.
+require('./tracing')
+require('./capture')
+```
+
+```js
+'use strict'
+// Jest setupFilesAfterEnv: every expect becomes an assertion note; when the test file ends, its spans become one
+// OTLP/JSON line of spans-<worker>.jsonl, and the process-wide subscriptions are undone so the next file in this
+// worker starts clean.
+const fs = require('node:fs')
+const path = require('node:path')
+const { JsonTraceSerializer } = require('@opentelemetry/otlp-transformer')
+const { exporter, fastifyOtel } = require('./tracing')
+const { stopCapture } = require('./capture')
+
+const dir = process.env.KRONIKOL_DIR ?? 'kronikol-capture'
+
+afterAll(() => {
+  const spans = exporter.getFinishedSpans()
+  if (spans.length > 0) {
+    fs.mkdirSync(dir, { recursive: true })
+    const line = Buffer.from(JsonTraceSerializer.serializeRequest(spans)).toString('utf8')
+    fs.appendFileSync(path.join(dir, `spans-${process.env.JEST_WORKER_ID ?? '1'}.jsonl`), line + '\n')
+  }
+  exporter.reset()
+  fastifyOtel.disable()
+  stopCapture()
+})
+
+// Each matcher call an assertion: its text the test's own line (expect(…).toBe(…)), its place that line's.
+const plain = text => String(text).replace(/\u001b\[[0-9;]*m/g, '')
+
+function callSite () {
+  for (const frame of new Error().stack.split('\n').slice(2)) {
+    const at = /\(?([^()\s]+):(\d+):\d+\)?$/.exec(frame)
+    if (!at || at[1].includes('node_modules') || at[1] === __filename || at[1].startsWith('node:')) continue
+    const line = fs.readFileSync(at[1], 'utf8').split('\n')[Number(at[2]) - 1]?.trim()
+    return { text: line, sourceFile: path.relative(process.cwd(), at[1]).split(path.sep).join('/'), sourceLine: Number(at[2]) }
+  }
+  return { text: 'assertion' }
+}
+
+function note (site, error) {
+  if (!globalThis.__kronikolTestId) return
+  fs.appendFileSync(path.join(dir, 'tests.ndjson'), JSON.stringify({
+    event: 'assertion', testId: globalThis.__kronikolTestId, attempt: globalThis.__kronikolAttempt, ...site,
+    status: error ? 'failed' : 'passed', error: error ? plain(error.message) : undefined, timestamp: new Date().toISOString()
+  }) + '\n')
+}
+
+function noting (matchers, site) {
+  return new Proxy(matchers, {
+    get (target, property, receiver) {
+      const value = Reflect.get(target, property, receiver)
+      if (property === 'not' || property === 'resolves' || property === 'rejects') return noting(value, site)
+      if (typeof value !== 'function') return value
+      return (...args) => {
+        let result
+        try {
+          result = value.apply(target, args)
+        } catch (error) {
+          note(site, error)
+          throw error
+        }
+        if (typeof result?.then !== 'function') {
+          note(site)
+          return result
+        }
+        return result.then(r => { note(site); return r }, error => { note(site, error); throw error })
+      }
+    }
+  })
+}
+
+globalThis.expect = new Proxy(globalThis.expect, {
+  apply: (target, thisArg, args) => noting(Reflect.apply(target, thisArg, args), callSite())
+})
+```
+
+A test that imports `expect` from `@jest/globals` gets Jest's own, which the wrapper does not reach. Every `expect`
+becomes a note in the diagram; the report's Show/Hide Assertions toggle hides them, or `note` can write failures
+only.
+
+## Handing the capture your MSW server
+
+The one line outside the harness: wherever the suite's MSW server is set up, pass it to `captureMsw`, with the names
+the diagram gives each faked host.
+
+```js
+const { captureMsw } = require('../kronikol/capture')
+captureMsw(msw, host => ({ 'psp.example.test': 'PSP' })[host] ?? 'Risk')
+```
+
+Name the service under test in the environment (`KRONIKOL_SERVICE=Payments GraphQL`); it is `Service` otherwise.
+
+## The CI step
+
+```bash
+NODE_OPTIONS=--experimental-vm-modules npx jest
+kronikol ingest kronikol-capture --tests kronikol-capture/tests.ndjson --spans kronikol-capture --separate-setup -o Reports
+```
+
+`--spans` given a directory takes the files in it that hold spans, so one folder holds all three kinds; the
+interaction captures are the rest. `--separate-setup` draws the calls made in `beforeEach` inside a Setup
+partition. The recipe's run printed:
+
+```text
+Spans: 87 span(s) from 1 file(s):
+Spans supplied to the ingest: 87 span(s).
+Replayed 32 interaction record(s) into 2 scenario(s).
+  Other: 8 interaction record(s) of earlier attempts of retried tests left out: a retried test's scenario shows its last attempt, with a retry label for each earlier one.
+  Other: 31 supplied span(s) are in no call's internal flow: 31 of a trace no call carries (graphql.parseSchema, graphql.parse, graphql.validate, …).
+```
+
+The 31 spans are the retried test's first attempt's (its calls were left out with it) and the three Fastify
+instances' schema parses at start-up, which belong to no call.
+
+## What the report shows
+
+- Each scenario's sequence diagram: `Test` → the service → `PSP` and `Risk`, a step bar per step, the `beforeEach`
+  call in a Setup partition, a ✓ note per `expect`.
+- On every arrow the internal-flow link: the GraphQL call's popup holds the request's 17 spans (Fastify's hooks and
+  handler, the GraphQL parse, validate and operation, the resolver and every field), as an activity diagram with a
+  swimlane per instrumentation (`@fastify/otel`, `@opentelemetry/instrumentation-graphql`) and as a flame chart; the
+  call to `PSP` shows the spans that ran in its time.
+- The scenario's Activity Diagrams and Flame Chart tabs: the test's whole flow, every span of its traces.
+- The retried test as its second attempt, labelled `retry 1`, with its source file.
+
+## Notes
+
+- **Running the service under Jest on Node 22** needed three things unrelated to Kronikol, which a suite that already
+  runs has solved: MSW pinned to 2.11 (later versions depend on the ESM-only `rettime`), `content-disposition`
+  overridden to 0.5.4, and `--experimental-vm-modules` (Mercurius loads a dependency with a dynamic `import()`).
+- **Clocks.** The harness's `Date.now()` and the spans' start times agreed to within a millisecond; internal flow
+  takes a span from 50 ms before a call's request to its response.
+- **Concurrency.** Tests of one file run one at a time, so the test id on the global is the running test's; the
+  `AsyncLocalStorage` scope keeps a request's test however late its work runs. `test.concurrent` would need the id
+  in a scope of its own.
+- **Named steps** (`step('When the card is charged')` between sections of a test, closing one step and opening the
+  next) are the one addition that touches tests; this recipe's steps are the lifecycle's.
+
